In this lesson, we'll look at rounding decimals and using estimation to sanity-check your answers. First up, the basic idea: rounding just shortens a number so it's easier to work with, without losing the big picture. The trick is to look at the digit right after the place you're rounding to. If that digit is five or more, you round up, otherwise you round down, and then drop everything to the right. A handy memory aid is "five or more, raise the score." Choosing the right accuracy matters too. Money usually rounds to the nearest cent. Things like paint or fencing, which you buy by the whole unit, should be rounded up so you don't run short. A renovation budget might only need the nearest hundred dollars, while scientific measurements should match the precision of your least-precise input. Finally, estimation is your reasonableness check. Round every number to one significant figure, do the maths mentally, and compare that rough answer to your real one. If they're wildly different, something's gone wrong, so redo it. Remember though, estimation is for checking, not for answering questions that ask for a specific accuracy. Now let's see it in action.